<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Baden</h1><p class="st-meta">1918 CE – 1945 CE · capital Karlsruhe</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Democratic successor of the Grand Duchy of Baden, a stable Weimar-era state on the French border, divided after 1945.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In November 1918 a provisional government was formed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlsruhe">Karlsruhe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii-grand-duke-of-baden">Grand Duke Frederick II</a> abdicated. In January 1919 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> elected a constituent assembly, one of the first German elections in which women could vote, and its constitution, approved by referendum in 1919, made Baden a democratic republic.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> was governed by a stable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> coalition of the Catholic Centre, Social Democrats and liberals. As a border state facing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, it suffered from the demilitarised zone along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and economic isolation. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">Nazis</a> took control in March 1933 under Reich governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-wagner-gauleiter">Robert Wagner</a>, who in 1940 deported Baden's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurs-internment-camp">Gurs</a> camp in France.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1945 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> was divided between the American zone in the north, joined to Württemberg-Baden, and the French zone in the south, which became the state of Baden. All were merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a> in 1952.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-baden.jpg" alt="Republic of Baden" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: David Liuzzo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flagge_Gro%C3%9Fherzogtum_Baden_(1891%E2%80%931918).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1918 CE – 1945 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Karlsruhe</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Robert Wagner</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Alemannic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republik Baden, Free State of Baden</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1932&amp;focus=republic-of-baden" aria-label="Republic of Baden on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4874 467 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Baden lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5194 723L5194 723M5194 723L5300 622M5194 723L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5194 723h0M5300 622h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5194 723h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5194 723h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Karlsruhe. <a href="/atlas?year=1932&amp;focus=republic-of-baden">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
